+    {
+      "key": "p0340-tree",
+      "namespace": "probability.randomtrees",
+      "logical_name": "full-binary-tree-drawn-length-height-identity",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Drawn length of a full binary tree: exact identity and depth bounds",
+      "statement": "For a finite rooted full binary tree T with n leaves and edge height h(T), define l(leaf)=0 and l(T)=l(L)+l(R)+2+|h(L)-h(R)|. If D(T) is the sum of leaf depths, then l(T)=sum over internal vertices v of h(T_v)+h(T), and 2(n-1)<=l(T)<=D(T). This is a deterministic lemma; independent audit is pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let T be a finite rooted full binary tree. Let n(T) be its number of leaves, h(T) its height in edges, D(T) the sum of the depths of its leaves, and l(T) the minimal drawn length defined by l(leaf)=0 and l(T)=l(L)+l(R)+2+|h(L)-h(R)|. Write S(T)=sum_{v internal} h(T_v).\n\nThen l(T)=S(T)+h(T) and 2(n(T)-1)<=l(T)<=D(T).\n\nProof. For a leaf all quantities are zero except n=1. At an internal root, h(T)=1+max(h(L),h(R)), so 2h(T)-h(L)-h(R)=2+|h(L)-h(R)|. Substituting the inductive identities into S(T)+h(T) proves the identity. The lower bound follows by summing the increment at least 2 over the n-1 internal vertices. Every full binary tree with k leaves has height at most k-1, by induction on its two children. Hence 2+|h(L)-h(R)|<=n(L)+n(R); combined with D(T)=D(L)+D(R)+n(L)+n(R), induction proves the upper bound.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "The tree is full binary, and the height of a leaf is zero."
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-probability-campaign-w343:p0340-tree",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w343/P0340_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-2026-10-10-pending-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign proof or status note)",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "p0340-height",
+      "namespace": "probability.randomtrees",
+      "logical_name": "critical-beta-splitting-height-geometric-moment-bound",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "An explicit expected maximum-height bound for critical beta-splitting trees",
+      "statement": "Let T_n be the independent recursive binary splitting tree with q_m(i)=m/[2H_(m-1)i(m-i)], where H_k=sum_(j=1)^k 1/j. For n>=2 put p=1/(2H_(n-1)), b=ceil(log_2 n), and theta=-log(1-p/2). Its expected edge height is at most (log n+b log 2)/theta <=4H_(n-1)(2 log n+log 2). Each tagged leaf depth is bounded by a sum of b geometric(p) waiting times, whose exponential moment at theta is 2^b. No independence among leaf depths is required. Author proof; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "The prior campaign proof supplies the full linear-limit argument, not just the leaf-depth inequality. The load-bearing estimates can be proved as follows, with a tighter geometric exponential moment.\n\nThe split law is q_m(i)=m/[2 H_{m-1} i(m-i)], where H denotes a harmonic number. A uniformly tagged leaf has next-clade size j with probability 1/[H_{m-1}(m-j)]. Its conditional probability of halving the clade size is at least 1/(2 H_{m-1}). For a chain started at n, put p_n=1/(2 H_{n-1}) and b_n=ceil(log_2 n). Couple successful halvings from below by independent Bernoulli(p_n) trials. Every tagged leaf depth is dominated by the waiting time for b_n successes, a sum of b_n geometric(p_n) variables on {1,2,...}.\n\nSet theta_n=-log(1-p_n/2). The geometric moment is exactly E exp(theta_n G)=2. A union bound in exponential-moment form over n leaf depths, followed by Jensen, gives\n\nE height(T_n) <= [log n+b_n log 2]/theta_n <= 4 H_{n-1}[2 log n+log 2] = O((log n)^2).\n\nNo independence among leaves is used: pointwise exp(theta_n h(T_n)) is at most the sum of exp(theta_n d) over its leaves, whose expected sum is n times the moment of a uniformly tagged leaf. Thus E exp(theta_n h(T_n))<=n 2^b_n before applying Jensen. Compared with the earlier proof's moment bound 3 and integrated tail, this removes its extra factor and integration term.\n\nLet a(n,m) be the probability the tagged descent chain visits m. In a dyadic block (b,2b], each step exits the block with conditional probability at least 1/(2 H_{2b-1}), and the chain never returns. Consequently sum_{m=b+1}^{2b} a(n,m)<=2 H_{2b-1}, uniformly in n. If h_m=E height(T_m), the block sum of a(n,m)h_m/m is at most C(1+log b)^3/b. Summing over dyadic b proves a uniform vanishing tail.\n\nThe Markov branching property and the exact clade identity E N_n(m)=n a(n,m)/m give E drawn(T_n)/n=sum_{m=2}^n a(n,m) h_m/m+h_n/n. The established pointwise occupation limit a(n,m)->a(m)=6 H_{m-1}/[pi^2(m-1)], finite-sum convergence, the uniform tail bound, and h_n/n->0 prove\n\nE drawn(T_n)/n -> c=sum_{m=2}^infinity a(m)h_m/m, with 2<=c<infinity.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Independent recursive subtrees conditional on split sizes.",
+        "Leaf height zero; natural logarithms except log_2."
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-probability-campaign-w343:p0340-height",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w343/P0340_proof.md",
