+    {
+      "key": "c0036-two",
+      "namespace": "probability.determinantal",
+      "logical_name": "two-site-equivariant-determinantal-sharp-product-domination",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Sharp product domination for two-site equivariant determinantal kernels",
+      "statement": "For a Hermitian positive contraction Q=[[a,z],[conj(z),a]], put p=sqrt(a^2-|z|^2) and q=1-sqrt((1-a)^2-|z|^2). Its determinantal law stochastically dominates iid Bernoulli(p) and is stochastically dominated by iid Bernoulli(q). Both parameters are optimal, including when Q or I-Q is singular. This proves only the two-site case of the general equivariant conjecture; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let Q be a Hermitian two-site equivariant positive contraction. Write its diagonal entries as a and its off-diagonal modulus as r; 0<=a-r<=a+r<=1. Put d=a^2-r^2=det Q and p=sqrt(d). Its determinantal measure has probabilities P(11)=d, P(10)=P(01)=a-d, P(00)=1-2a+d.\n\nIt stochastically dominates Bernoulli(p) on two sites. All nontrivial increasing events are: site 1 present; site 2 present; both present; at least one present. Their probabilities are respectively a,a,d,2a-d. The product probabilities are p,p,p^2,2p-p^2. Since p<=a and p^2=d, all four comparisons hold. This works at spectral endpoints as well. Applying the same argument to I-Q and complementing gives the upper bound Bernoulli(1-sqrt(det(I-Q))). The constants are optimal, by the both-present and both-absent events.\n\nFor any finite transitive equivariant contraction on N sites, an iid lower domination density b necessarily satisfies b^N<=det Q, hence b<=det(Q)^(1/N). An iid upper domination density c necessarily satisfies (1-c)^N<=det(I-Q), hence c>=1-det(I-Q)^(1/N). This proves necessity, not general sufficiency. Equivariance is needed to identify these determinants' Nth roots with the finite-group Fuglede-Kadison determinant (normalized trace).\n\nEarlier-route check: Q=[[1/2,1/4],[1/4,1/2]] has eigenvalues 1/4 and 3/4. Its harmonic mean is 3/8, whereas its geometric mean is sqrt(3)/4. The smallest full-rest-conditioned presence probability is 3/8. Thus the full-rest one-site lower-bound route cannot certify the sharp geometric-mean density, even though the stochastic domination itself holds. The Schur complement is odds s, and the probability is s/(1+s); they must not be conflated.\n\nSource correspondence: Lyons, Determinantal Probability: Basic Properties and Conjectures (arXiv:1406.2707), Conjecture 5.7, and intake C0036-A002. No result here proves the conjecture for arbitrary groups or proves its current literature status.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "0<=a-|z|<=a+|z|<=1."
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-probability-campaign-w343:c0036-two",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w343/C0036_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-2026-10-10-pending-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign proof or status note)",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "c0036-necessary",
+      "namespace": "probability.determinantal",
+      "logical_name": "finite-determinantal-product-domination-determinant-necessity",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Necessary finite determinant bounds for iid domination",
+      "statement": "For a determinantal probability law with positive-contraction kernel Q on N>=1 sites, iid lower stochastic domination at density b implies b<=det(Q)^(1/N); iid upper stochastic domination at density c implies c>=1-det(I-Q)^(1/N). The all-present and all-absent events prove these inequalities. For a finite transitive equivariant kernel these roots are the normalized Fuglede-Kadison determinants. Necessity does not imply the conjectured general sufficiency; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let Q be a Hermitian two-site equivariant positive contraction. Write its diagonal entries as a and its off-diagonal modulus as r; 0<=a-r<=a+r<=1. Put d=a^2-r^2=det Q and p=sqrt(d). Its determinantal measure has probabilities P(11)=d, P(10)=P(01)=a-d, P(00)=1-2a+d.\n\nIt stochastically dominates Bernoulli(p) on two sites. All nontrivial increasing events are: site 1 present; site 2 present; both present; at least one present. Their probabilities are respectively a,a,d,2a-d. The product probabilities are p,p,p^2,2p-p^2. Since p<=a and p^2=d, all four comparisons hold. This works at spectral endpoints as well. Applying the same argument to I-Q and complementing gives the upper bound Bernoulli(1-sqrt(det(I-Q))). The constants are optimal, by the both-present and both-absent events.\n\nFor any finite transitive equivariant contraction on N sites, an iid lower domination density b necessarily satisfies b^N<=det Q, hence b<=det(Q)^(1/N). An iid upper domination density c necessarily satisfies (1-c)^N<=det(I-Q), hence c>=1-det(I-Q)^(1/N). This proves necessity, not general sufficiency. Equivariance is needed to identify these determinants' Nth roots with the finite-group Fuglede-Kadison determinant (normalized trace).\n\nEarlier-route check: Q=[[1/2,1/4],[1/4,1/2]] has eigenvalues 1/4 and 3/4. Its harmonic mean is 3/8, whereas its geometric mean is sqrt(3)/4. The smallest full-rest-conditioned presence probability is 3/8. Thus the full-rest one-site lower-bound route cannot certify the sharp geometric-mean density, even though the stochastic domination itself holds. The Schur complement is odds s, and the probability is s/(1+s); they must not be conflated.\n\nSource correspondence: Lyons, Determinantal Probability: Basic Properties and Conjectures (arXiv:1406.2707), Conjecture 5.7, and intake C0036-A002. No result here proves the conjecture for arbitrary groups or proves its current literature status.",
+      "assumptions": [],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-probability-campaign-w343:c0036-necessary",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w343/C0036_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-2026-10-10-pending-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign proof or status note)",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "c0036-conditioning",
+      "namespace": "probability.determinantal",
+      "logical_name": "two-site-harmonic-geometric-conditioning-gap",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Full-rest conditionals need not reach the sharp geometric-mean density",
+      "statement": "For Q=[[1/2,1/4],[1/4,1/2]], the minimum presence probability conditioned on the other site is 3/8, while the sharp iid lower domination density is sqrt(3)/4. Thus a worst-full-rest-conditional lower-bound method does not certify the geometric-mean density. The relevant Schur complement is odds s; its presence probability is s/(1+s), not s. Independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let Q be a Hermitian two-site equivariant positive contraction. Write its diagonal entries as a and its off-diagonal modulus as r; 0<=a-r<=a+r<=1. Put d=a^2-r^2=det Q and p=sqrt(d). Its determinantal measure has probabilities P(11)=d, P(10)=P(01)=a-d, P(00)=1-2a+d.\n\nIt stochastically dominates Bernoulli(p) on two sites. All nontrivial increasing events are: site 1 present; site 2 present; both present; at least one present. Their probabilities are respectively a,a,d,2a-d. The product probabilities are p,p,p^2,2p-p^2. Since p<=a and p^2=d, all four comparisons hold. This works at spectral endpoints as well. Applying the same argument to I-Q and complementing gives the upper bound Bernoulli(1-sqrt(det(I-Q))). The constants are optimal, by the both-present and both-absent events.\n\nFor any finite transitive equivariant contraction on N sites, an iid lower domination density b necessarily satisfies b^N<=det Q, hence b<=det(Q)^(1/N). An iid upper domination density c necessarily satisfies (1-c)^N<=det(I-Q), hence c>=1-det(I-Q)^(1/N). This proves necessity, not general sufficiency. Equivariance is needed to identify these determinants' Nth roots with the finite-group Fuglede-Kadison determinant (normalized trace).\n\nEarlier-route check: Q=[[1/2,1/4],[1/4,1/2]] has eigenvalues 1/4 and 3/4. Its harmonic mean is 3/8, whereas its geometric mean is sqrt(3)/4. The smallest full-rest-conditioned presence probability is 3/8. Thus the full-rest one-site lower-bound route cannot certify the sharp geometric-mean density, even though the stochastic domination itself holds. The Schur complement is odds s, and the probability is s/(1+s); they must not be conflated.\n\nSource correspondence: Lyons, Determinantal Probability: Basic Properties and Conjectures (arXiv:1406.2707), Conjecture 5.7, and intake C0036-A002. No result here proves the conjecture for arbitrary groups or proves its current literature status.",
+      "assumptions": [],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
