+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "p0150-whisker",
+      "namespace": "probability.metric",
+      "logical_name": "uniform-star-whisker-gromov-weak-nontightness",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Uniform finite graphs can hide a diverging pendant diameter",
+      "statement": "Attach floor(sqrt(m)) new path vertices to the center of an m-vertex star, give all vertices uniform probability mass, and rescale graph distance by m^(-1/4). Every fixed iid sampled distance matrix tends in probability to zero, since the path mass tends to zero and star distances are at most 2m^(-1/4). The rescaled diameter is (floor(sqrt(m))+1)m^(-1/4), which diverges. Thus Gromov-weak convergence to a point need not imply GH tightness even for uniform full-support finite graphs. This family is not a heavy-tailed critical configuration model; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let K_m be a star with m vertices. Attach a pendant path with k_m=floor(sqrt(m)) new vertices to its center. Give all m+k_m vertices uniform mass and rescale graph distance by a_m=m^(-1/4).\n\nFor any fixed r independent samples, the probability that at least one sample is in the path is at most r k_m/(m+k_m), which tends to zero. If all samples belong to the star, every distance is at most 2a_m, which tends to zero. Thus all finite sampled distance matrices converge in probability to the zero matrix: the spaces converge Gromov-weakly to a point.\n\nTheir rescaled diameters equal a_m(k_m+1) for m>=2 and tend to infinity. The Gromov-Hausdorff distance to a point is half the diameter, so they fail even GH tightness. The uniform measure has full support in every finite graph. This is a graph version of P0150-A001's thin-mass obstruction, without an atom-plus-continuous measure. It is not a configuration-model counterexample and does not meet the heavy-tailed critical degree hypotheses.",
+      "assumptions": [],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
