+    {
+      "key": "c0060-fiber",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "finite-cut-vertex-fiber-hits-product-bypass",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Finite cut-vertex fiber hits do not separate a product graph",
+      "statement": "Let c be a cut vertex of a connected graph G and let a,b lie on opposite sides. Any finite set F contained in {c} x Z can be bypassed in G x Z by moving to a height not used by F, crossing via c there, and returning along the other side. For the three-vertex path a-c-b, removing (c,z) for |z|<=H leaves a shortest a_0-to-b_0 path of length 2H+4. Hitting a corner region alone is not a proof of separation. This does not determine the vacant-set threshold of an infinite interlacement trace; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let G be connected, let c be a cut vertex, and let a,b lie on different sides with finite G-paths to c. Let F be any finite subset of {c} x Z. Choose a height z whose copy (c,z) is not in F. The path from (a,s) vertically to (a,z), then along a-c-b at height z, then vertically to (b,t), avoids F. Thus a finite set of hits on a cut-point fiber does not separate its two sides in G x Z.\n\nFor the three-vertex path a-c-b, deleting {(c,z): |z|<=H} still leaves a path from (a,0) to (b,0) of length 2H+4, going through height H+1. It is shortest because every crossing must use a middle vertex with |z|>=H+1, requiring at least 2(H+1) vertical and two horizontal edges.\n\nThis refutes the inference 'the interlacement hits every corner region, therefore it blocks every crossing' used heuristically in C0060-A001. Hitting a region and creating a separating occupied set are different events. The interlacement trace is infinite; this finite-fiber example does not determine its vacant threshold, its crossing probabilities, or u*. No isotropic capacity exponent from the earlier heuristic is used as a theorem here.",
