+    {
+      "key": "c0085-sublevel",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "invasion-explores-finite-root-sublevel-cluster",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Invasion explores a finite root sublevel cluster before exiting it",
+      "statement": "On a connected countable locally finite infinite weighted graph, invasion starts at o and repeatedly adds the other endpoint of a minimum-weight boundary edge. If the ordinary weight-at-most-p root cluster C_p(o) is finite, invasion explores all of it before selecting its first edge with weight greater than p. Hence, under iid uniform weights and almost-sure finiteness of C_pc(o), P(x in IPC)>=P_pc(o connected to x). This compares with an ordinary critical cluster, not the incipient infinite cluster; it does not yield the target invasion exponent d-4. Independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Give every edge of a connected, countable, locally finite infinite graph a weight. Invasion percolation starts at o and repeatedly selects a minimum-weight edge with exactly one endpoint in its current finite vertex set, adding the other endpoint. Fix p and suppose the ordinary sublevel cluster C_p(o), formed by edges of weight at most p, is finite.\n\nBefore that cluster is fully invaded, a path in C_p(o) from o to an uninvaded vertex supplies a boundary edge of weight at most p. Therefore invasion cannot choose an edge of weight greater than p yet. Any boundary edge with weight at most p ends inside C_p(o). Each invasion step adds a new vertex, so exactly |C_p(o)|-1 such steps explore all of C_p(o) before the first weight greater than p is selected. In particular C_p(o) is contained in the eventual invasion cluster.\n\nFor iid uniform edge weights, if C_pc(o) is finite almost surely, this yields P(x in IPC)>=P_pc(o connected to x). If an ordinary critical two-point lower bound of order |x|^(2-d) is available, the same lower bound follows for invasion. It is two powers weaker than the target |x|^(4-d) and does not compare IPC with the incipient infinite cluster. The finiteness assumption is explicit; an infinite sublevel cluster requires a separate argument.\n\nThis continues C0085-A001's failed IIC-comparison route using an ordinary percolation cluster instead. It neither identifies the invasion asymptotic constant nor proves the high-dimensional conjecture.",
