+    {
+      "key": "c0131-pathwise",
+      "namespace": "probability.randomenvironment",
+      "logical_name": "uniform-elliptic-adapted-walk-pathwise-site-dichotomy",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Uniform ellipticity gives a pathwise all-or-none recurrence alternative",
+      "statement": "For an adapted nearest-neighbor walk on a connected countable graph, suppose each neighbor transition has conditional probability at least kappa>0 given the complete past. Almost surely, either every vertex is visited infinitely often or every vertex is visited only finitely often. If u has infinitely many visits, the chance of missing a fixed neighbor at k successive opportunities is at most (1-kappa)^k; propagation along finite paths proves the claim. On Z^d the second alternative leaves every finite ball eventually. This does not make the first alternative's annealed probability zero or one. Independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Consider any adapted nearest-neighbor walk on a connected countable graph. Suppose that, at every step, conditional on its complete past, the probability of moving to each neighbor of the current vertex is at least a fixed kappa>0. This applies to uniformly elliptic cookie walks without using iid stacks.\n\nIf u is visited infinitely often, then each neighbor v is also visited infinitely often almost surely. To see this, after any fixed time choose successive visits to u. Conditional on the past at each visit, failure to move next to v has probability at most 1-kappa. The probability that there are k such opportunities but none is used is at most (1-kappa)^k, by iterated conditioning at their stopping times. If u has infinitely many visits but v only finitely many, after some integer time every opportunity fails; letting k grow rules this out. Union over integer times and the countably many oriented edges, then propagate along finite paths.\n\nThus almost every sample path either visits every vertex infinitely often or visits every vertex only finitely often. On Z^d the latter is equivalent to eventually leaving every finite ball. The remaining C0131 question is whether the annealed probability of the recurrent alternative must be zero or one under the specified iid cookie law. This pathwise lemma supplies no such law.",
+      "assumptions": [],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
