+    {
+      "key": "p0046-columns",
+      "namespace": "probability.spanningforests",
+      "logical_name": "vertical-line-forest-sprinkling-window-connectivity",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "A vertical-line forest admits every positive horizontal sprinkling",
+      "statement": "In Z^2 let F consist of all vertical edges. Every 0<delta<1 independent edge sprinkling connects its columns almost surely. In a rectangular window of q>=1 columns and r>=1 horizontal levels, retaining its vertical forest, the exact connectivity probability is [1-(1-delta)^r]^(q-1). Adjacent column pairs have disjoint r-edge interfaces. This example is a spanning forest, not an assertion about the FUSF law; independent audit pending.",
+      "proof_text": "Let G be a connected countable locally finite graph and F a random spanning forest. Conditional on F, form a graph H on the F-components, joining two distinct components when infinitely many G-edges run between them. Suppose H is connected almost surely (a single component is permitted). Then for any 0<delta<1, F united with independent Bernoulli(delta) G-edges is connected almost surely.\n\nProof. For any given adjacent pair in H, the probability that none of its first k boundary edges is added is (1-delta)^k, which tends to zero. There are at most countably many pairs of components; conditional on F, almost surely every edge of H has at least one added representative. A finite H-path between any two F-components therefore lifts to a finite G-path using F-edges and added representatives. This proves connectivity. If the law of F is invariant under the given graph action, independent identical edge sprinkling preserves that invariance. Every edge is added outside F with probability delta P(e not in F)<=delta. Choose delta<epsilon for the strict inequality requested in P0046.\n\nExample: in Z^2 take F to be all vertical edges. Its components are columns, adjacent columns have infinitely many horizontal boundary edges, and H is the line. Every positive sprinkling connects F. In a window of 2H+1 horizontal levels with q columns, the exact connectivity probability is [1-(1-delta)^(2H+1)]^(q-1).\n\nThe hypothesis is not established for a general FUSF. The source explicitly notes that independent sprinkling fails on some nonamenable graphs, so this criterion is only a sufficient special case and a diagnostic for the missing interface structure. It does not solve Question 10.12 or the associated cost-equality problem.",
